  "slug": "mitochondrial-resource-16ed",
  "summary": "Long-read assemblies now resolve telomeres, hyper-divergent regions and sex chromosomes in Plasmodium, Caenorhabditis, Gambusia and Hordeum genomes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of long-read de novo assembly to generate chromosome-scale or near-complete genomes, which are then interrogated for variation. The Malian Plasmodium falciparum study applies an optimized PacBio HiFi strategy to eight cloned field isolates to recover near-complete genomes. The Caenorhabditis elegans pangenome spans 140 strains and links copy-number variation in hyper-divergent regions to an abamectin resistance locus. The Gambusia holbrooki reference combines HiFi and Hi-C data to identify a newly evolved W/Z sex chromosome pair in the invasive range. The Hordeum resource is a chromosome-scale comparative genomic dataset covering all diploid species and polyploids, addressing structural variation, reticulate evolution and horizontal gene transfer. What is new is the breadth of taxa and the use of assemblies to resolve lineage-specific structural and gene-content features, rather than serving only as reference sequences.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
